From 59c1534660ea94306aae23bf1e977ec6beb43478 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dispatch Sandbox <jax1313@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 19:15:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 04/12] Make multi-step service operations atomic, and defer
 sends past the commit

Sixteen service operations wrote more than once, and every store call opened,
committed and closed a connection of its own. add_milestone() wrote the
milestone in one transaction, the load's new status in a second and the
visibility record in a third, so a crash, a lock timeout, or a failure partway
through left a milestone recorded against a load whose status never advanced --
no rollback, no reconciliation pass, and nothing on any screen saying the two
disagreed. On a laptop that gets closed mid-write, that window opens routinely.

@atomic is the whole change at each call site, because db.unit_of_work() makes
nested get_connection() calls join one transaction rather than open their own.
No store function needed an argument, and none needed to know it was sharing.

Notifications move behind db.after_commit(). Once these became atomic, sending
inside one would have been wrong twice over: an email must not go out for work
that then rolled back -- a delivery announced to a broker for a milestone the
database discarded is the worst failure here and it is invisible -- and a
thirty-second SMTP timeout must not be served while holding a write lock every
other writer is queued behind.

tests/test_atomic_service_operations.py injects a failure after the first
successful write in each operation and asserts the first write is gone too.
